    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where Wix is the right call</h2>
    <p>Wix is a good product, and it's the right call at the experiment stage: a brand-new business, a model that may change in three months, and a need to be live this weekend. Custom code is the wrong tool for that stage. Use Wix, and come back when the business is settled and the leads justify a custom site.</p>

    <h2>Where Wix costs you leads</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Slower pages.</strong> Wix sites typically score 50&ndash;70 on Google's speed test for phones (<span data-term="Google's test of how fast your site loads, scored 0 to 100 on phones and on computers. A score of 95 or higher is the target today.">PageSpeed</span>); a custom-coded site scores 95&ndash;100. Each extra second of loading loses roughly 7&ndash;10% of phone visitors. At 500 phone visits a month, that's dozens of calls a year.</li>
      <li><strong>Weaker local search.</strong> Wix fills in the business details Google reads (<span data-term="Structured data added to a page so search engines can understand your business: name, address, hours, services, and reviews. Google's rich results depend on it.">schema</span>) from a template, and its city pages look like every other Wix city page in your trade. Google increasingly treats look-alike pages as low effort and rewards a unique page for each area in its map results (the "local pack").</li>
      <li><strong>Simpler forms.</strong> The Wix form doesn't sort repairs from installs, flag urgent jobs, or connect cleanly to customer-tracking software (a CRM). A form that asks the right three questions turns more visitors into qualified leads.</li>
      <li><strong>A shared look.</strong> Wix's starter templates power tens of thousands of sites, likely including your competitors'. In search results, standing out decides who gets the click.</li>
    </ul>

